WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.600
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.589
If you're an avid Easter Egg hunter, like
me, then prepare for a full-on geekgasm as

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:10.980
Captain America: Civil War has oodles to keep
you entertained.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.750
In this video, I'm gonna run through all the
easter eggs, references, and cameos I spotted

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:15.750
in the movie.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.940
Of course, I'd love to hear about any others
you noticed too.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.310
Just before that, I've got an exciting giveaway
thanks to DK Publishers, so if you'd like

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:28.050
the chance to win this amazing "Captain America:
The Ultimate Guide to the First Avenger" hardback

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.679
book packed with tons of cool info and images
about Cap's 75-year history in Marvel comics

00:00:33.679 --> 00:00:39.030
as well as an intro from Stan Lee himself,
then make sure you subscribe and leave a comment

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.129
about your favourite easter egg from the movie.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:45.269
For more ways to enter, click the Gleam link
in the video description!

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:49.989
And remember, there are spoilers ahead, so
if you want to avoid those, you can watch

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:51.899
my spoiler-free review here!

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:57.020
Although the movie isn't a direct adaptation
of Marvel's Civil War comic book series, there

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.089
are some cool visual homages to iconic covers
and pages from the comics.

00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:05.189
This image of Iron Man blasting against Captain
America's shield comes from the final issue

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:07.040
of the main Civil War series.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.890
And when Captain America smashes Iron Man's
mask and looks like he's about to finish off

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:15.290
Tony Stark, it's incredibly similar to a moment
from the final battle between Cap and Iron

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.080
Man in the comic books.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.630
And during that final fight scene, Captain
America's line to Tony "I could do this all

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.899
day" echoes the same words he said while fighting
off a bully in an alley in Captain America:

00:01:25.899 --> 00:01:31.469
The First Avenger and again to Red Skull at
the end of the same movie.

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:35.310
Also in that scene, there's a fresh hint that
Bucky could one day take up Captain America's

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.590
mantle in the movies as he does in the comics,
when Cap and Bucky pass the shield back and

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.450
forwards to each other as they batter Iron
Man with it.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:47.450
There's been a nod to Bucky becoming Captain
America in each Captain America movie so far.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.320
In the first movie, Bucky picks up Cap's shield
while they're fighting on the train carrying

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.450
Zola, and in The Winter Soldier, Bucky grabs
the shield when Rogers is chasing him after

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.979
Bucky shot Nick Fury.

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.869
In the movie's stairway fight scene, you can
also see the influence of Gareth Evans' The

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.310
Raid action movies, which also influenced
Season 1 and Season 2 of Marvel's Daredevil

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.320
on Netflix.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:11.530
The MCU's homage to Star Wars arm loss that
started in Phase 2 continues into Phase 3

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.610
with Bucky losing his metal arm when Iron
Man's reactor blasts it off in that final

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:16.610
fight scene.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.250
When Tony Stark calls Bucky 'Manchurian Candidate',
it's a reference to the novel and film of

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.300
the same name about a man who's brainwashed
into becoming an assassin.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.030
Although Stark has a history of being grabbed
by the throat in Marvel's movies, this time

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:34.170
he appears to escape, with Falcon, Black Widow,
Cap, Black Panther, and Maria Stark all getting

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.140
throttled by Bucky Barnes instead!

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.000
Oh, and Black Panther almost got to grab Bucky's
throat with his claws, until Scarlet Witch

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.000
stepped in.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.810
The cell Bucky's held in is labelled D-23,
a hidden reference to Disney's official fan

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:53.550
club D23 with 23 referencing the year 1923
that Walt Disney founded the Walt Disney Company.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:58.400
The activation codes that Zemo uses to trigger
Bucky have some cool hidden references too.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.420
The numbers ONE – NINE and SEVENTEEN form
the year 1917, which is when Bucky Barnes

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.320
was born according to the museum exhibit in
the Winter Soldier's post-credits scene.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.760
"Freight Car" is likely a reference to the
train freight car Bucky fell from in Captain

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.610
America: The First Avenger, and "Homecoming"
is a nod to the title of Spider-Man's 2017

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:16.610
solo movie.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.670
And the word "Daybreak" is likely a sneaky
reference to the TV show Community.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.120
Daybreak being a jazz melody which featured
in multiple episodes of the show.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.511
Both Civil War directors Anthony and Joe Russo
directed and produced numerous episodes of

00:03:29.511 --> 00:03:30.960
the comedy series.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.410
By the way, there's another nod to Community
in the cameo by Jim Rash who played Dean Pelton

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:36.730
in the TV show.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:41.440
In Civil War, Rash is the MIT liaison who
tries to get funding from Tony Stark for his

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.370
self-cooking hot dog idea.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:48.210
The mission report date that Zemo is hunting
for, December 16 1991, references the day

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:52.710
that Bucky assassinated Howard and Maria Stark,
with the news report we see in Captain America:

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.400
The Winter Soldier being published the next
day.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:00.510
Coincidentally, 16th December 1991 is also
when Marvel published its very first annual

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.260
report to shareholders, which was actually
in the form of a comic book!

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.400
And curiously the movie's working title, "Sputnik",
is a clever easter egg to what happens to

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.400
Bucky at the very end of the film.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.790
In the comics, "Sputnik" is the safeword the
Russians implanted in Bucky as a safety mechanism

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.959
to shut him down in case he went crazy.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.540
In the post-credits, Bucky is shut down again
when he volunteers to be frozen until someone

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.500
can figure out how to remove his HYDRA programming.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.411
Bucky getting frozen in Wakanda also recalls
the flashback scene in The Winter Soldier

00:04:28.411 --> 00:04:32.960
where Zola orders Bucky to be put on ice and
is also a nod to the Captain America comics

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.560
where the Soviet general Vasily Karpov decides
to put Bucky back into cryostasis while they

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.280
figure out what to do with him.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.750
That same Russian general Vasily Karpov from
the comics appears in the movie as the HYDRA

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.380
agent who Zemo drowns in Cleveland.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.032
And if you're wondering what Karpov was doing
hanging out in Cleveland, well, it's probably

00:04:50.032 --> 00:04:53.540
a little tribute to the Ohio city in which
the Civil War directors Anthony and Joe Russo

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.710
grew up.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.690
When Cap pulls Bucky out of the water after
their helicopter battle, it's a nice reversal

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.620
of the way Bucky pulled Cap out of the water
after their battle on the helicarrier at the

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.760
end of The Winter Soldier.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:09.630
After his return from The Winter Soldier movie,
Brock Rumlow references the Battle at the

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.380
Triskelion when he accuses Captain America
of "dropping a building on my face".

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.950
To hide his burned and scarred face, Rumlow
returns masked and with his trademark Crossbones

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.840
uniform and weaponised gauntlets that the
villain has in the comics.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.350
Falcon's sidekick drone Redwing is the MCU's
version of the real falcon Redwing from the

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.210
comics who Sam Wilson communicated with telepathically.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.330
In the movie, that telepathic link has been
replaced with a tech communications interface.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.302
And there's a couple more comic book Falcon
easter eggs in the movie too.

00:05:37.302 --> 00:05:43.150
The Sokovia accords which were approved by
117 countries reference Captain America issue

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:47.220
117 which was Falcon and Redwing's first appearance
in the series.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.480
At the airport one of the walkways is labelled
134, an easter egg to Captain America Vol

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.030
1 issue 134 when Sam Wilson officially teams
up with Cap and the comic series is renamed

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:02.750
"Captain America and the Falcon" for over
80 issues.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.490
And the airport fight scene is chock full
of easter eggs.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:09.590
When Iron Man asks Hawkeye about being retired
and playing golf it's a funny wink to the

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:10.590
What If?

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:14.190
comic series where Hawkeye briefly took up
golf rather than archery.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:18.130
And the joke about Hawkeye missing when he
shoots at Iron Man is also a nod to the Hawkeye

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.220
Blindspot comics, where Hawkeye shoots and
misses a gang of villains.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:27.810
Hawkeye shooting Ant-Man from his arrow references
Avengers #223 when Hawkeye shot Ant-Man from

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:31.770
his arrow in a fight against Taskmaster.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:36.710
The fantastic scene when Scott Lang transforms
into Giant-Man is a nod to the time Hank Pym

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.860
transformed into Giant-Man for the first time
in the comics.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:44.080
And the hilarious Star Wars joke by Spider-Man
when he takes down Giant-Man by looping webbing

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.590
around his legs, of course, references The
Empire Strikes Back movie when the Rebel Alliance

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:52.110
used tow cables to entangle and defeat an
AT-AT.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:56.550
And Giant-Man swatting at the superheroes
flying around his head has shades of the Godzilla

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.360
and King Kong movies about it.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.050
Spider-Man nabbing Cap's shield at the airport
with his webbing is an easter egg to the time

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.940
he did that in the Civil War series, and at
the end of the fight when Peter Parker is

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:11.790
partially unmasked, it's a little nod to how
Spider-Man took off his mask to reveal his

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.400
identity in the Civil War comics.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.000
And when Spider-Man webs Captain America's
legs and says Tony told him to, it's a little

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.910
acknowledgement by the Russos of criticism
at the way gunmen in The Winter Soldier movie

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.199
kept on shooting at Cap's shield rather than
trying to take out his legs.

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:31.340
During the fight, Cap smashes a walkway so
that it falls onto Spider-Man who has to use

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:32.919
all his strength to hold it up.

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:37.190
It's a nice homage to the moment in The Amazing
Spider-Man comics when Spider-Man lifted tons

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:40.310
of steel that fell on him while fighting Dr
Octopus.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.880
And with Spider-Man making his debut in the
MCU, there's a stack more references and easter

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.880
eggs.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.700
Spidey's web-shooters are a mechanical invention
of his own rather than the organic shooters

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.350
we saw in the first Spider-Man trilogy.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:56.080
Spider-Man's origin gets just a brief reference
when Peter Parker says to Tony Stark "when

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.930
whatever happened, happened" and there's a
cryptic allusion to a possible tragedy involving

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:04.810
Uncle Ben when Peter says "when you can do
what you do and you don't and then bad things

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:05.960
happen, it's on you."

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:10.650
There's also a hint to the famous Spider-Man
phrase "with great power comes great responsibility"

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:15.060
when Peter mentions that even though he'd
love to play football now he has his powers,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.870
because he couldn't before that, he shouldn't
do so now.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.912
When Peter says to Tony about the YouTube
videos he's seen of Spider-Man "that's all

00:08:19.912 --> 00:08:23.919
fake, it's all done on a computer" it's a
reference to the fake Spider-Man post-credits

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:27.340
scene for Avengers Age of Ultron that went
viral on YouTube.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:31.090
And when Tony Stark says about Aunt May, "it's
so hard for me to believe she's someone's

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:35.810
aunt", Marisa Tomei's reply that "we come
in all shapes and sizes" is a reference to

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:40.990
the previous movie and comic-book incarnations
of Aunt May as well as a riposte to the backlash

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.279
over Tomei being cast in the role.

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:47.790
Tony and May flirting with each is another
reference to Robert Downey Jr and Marisa Tomei's

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:52.019
real-life relationship in the 90s and also
to their on-screen relationship in the rom-com

00:08:52.019 --> 00:08:53.379
"Only You".

00:08:53.379 --> 00:08:56.820
By the way, they also starred in the biopic
Chaplin together.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.869
And later back at Peter Parker's apartment
in the post-credits scene, Aunt May asks Peter

00:09:00.869 --> 00:09:05.579
about the fight he got into and if it's Steve
from 12-C, which could be a little easter

00:09:05.579 --> 00:09:08.790
egg to the price of the first Spider-Man comic
which cost 12 cents.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.920
During that post-credits scene, we also get
to see a new piece of tech that Peter discovers

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:14.980
while fiddling with the shooter around his
wrist.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:19.090
The device which projects a very cool Spidey
symbol on to the ceiling is very much like

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:23.319
the Spider signal that Spider-Man has attached
to his belt in the comics, and it seems likely

00:09:23.319 --> 00:09:26.550
that this is some new tech designed by Tony
Stark.

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:32.100
Iron Man himself also has some cool upgraded
tech, for example his portable gauntlet which

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.100
unfolds out of his watch is a similar example
to the miniaturisation of his whole suit which

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.930
we saw in Iron Man 2.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:42.290
Maria Stark's appearance during Tony's September
Foundation speech at MIT, where he studied

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.850
as a young man, is likely a nod to the Maria
Stark Foundation that Tony Stark set up in

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:46.910
the comics.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:51.009
During that scene at MIT, when the young Tony
Stark mentions the Pentagon, it's a call-back

00:09:51.009 --> 00:09:55.300
to Avengers Age of Ultron when Tony said he'd
hacked into the Pentagon as a dare at high

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:56.300
school.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.490
The grieving mother Miriam who lost her son
in Sokovia and who confronts Tony Stark and

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:04.600
proves to be the catalyst for him to support
the Sokovia Accords is based on the character

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:08.720
Miriam Sharpe from the comics who publicly
confronted Tony Stark about her son who was

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.730
killed in the Stamford explosion.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:15.360
Alfre Woodard, the actress who plays the mother,
is also set to play Mariah Dillard in Marvel's

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.680
upcoming Luke Cage series on Netflix, and
in the comics Mariah Dillard is the cousin

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:18.680
of the villain Cottonmouth.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.410
By the way, the name of her dead son, Charlie
Spencer, is actually a nod to Robert Downey

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:26.829
Jr's role as Charles Spencer Chaplin in the
1992 biopic Chaplin.

00:10:26.829 --> 00:10:30.839
And Tony Stark's comment that Charlie Spencer,
'didn't go to Vegas or Fort Lauderdale, which

00:10:30.839 --> 00:10:34.689
I'd do' is a reference to the first Iron Man
movie where Tony spends his time gambling

00:10:34.689 --> 00:10:38.829
at the casino while he should be receiving
an award, and to Iron Man 3 which filmed in

00:10:38.829 --> 00:10:40.550
the Fort Lauderdale area.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:44.300
When Tony goes to visit the Avengers who are
locked up in the Raft, Clint Barton calls

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:48.879
Tony Stark "the futurist", a nod to the various
times Iron Man has referred to himself as

00:10:48.879 --> 00:10:50.800
a futurist in the comics.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.779
Tony Stark says about the prison that "I didn't
know they'd put you in some floating supermax

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:58.779
ocean pokey; this place is for maniacs", which
is a reference to the The Raft prison from

00:10:58.779 --> 00:11:02.569
the comics, based on Rikers Island, and which
housed some of the worst super-villains from

00:11:02.569 --> 00:11:04.089
the Marvel universe.

00:11:04.089 --> 00:11:09.309
Scott Lang also blames Tony at the Raft saying
"like Pym said, you can never trust a Stark",

00:11:09.309 --> 00:11:13.680
a call-back to Ant-Man's movie when Hank Pym
explained to Scott how he's spent half his

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.279
life trying to keep his technology out of
the hands of a Stark.

00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:22.139
And when Tony Stark replies "who are you?",
Scott replies "come on, man", a funny echo

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:26.949
from Guardians of the Galaxy when Star-Lord
was frustrated that Korath didn't know who

00:11:26.949 --> 00:11:28.089
he was.

00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:32.329
Tony Stark's electromagnetic headache is a
nod to the time in the Iron Man comics when

00:11:32.329 --> 00:11:36.350
Rhodey donned the Iron Man costume and suffered
from headaches because the suit had been tailored

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.279
to match Tony Stark's brainwaves.

00:11:38.279 --> 00:11:41.890
And when Tony is helping Rhodes get back the
use of his legs at the end of the movie, it's

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:45.889
a reference to the comics when Tony Stark
built new bionic limbs for War Machine after

00:11:45.889 --> 00:11:47.810
he lost them in a terrorist attack.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:52.470
Iron Man's AI interface FRIDAY who replaced
JARVIS after he became Vision in Age of Ultron

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:56.879
returns with Tony saying he imagined her as
a redhead, a little nod to both the Invincible

00:11:56.879 --> 00:12:01.059
Iron Man comics in which she appeared as a
redhead and also to Pepper Potts.

00:12:01.059 --> 00:12:05.369
Speaking of Pepper Potts, her absence from
the movie is referenced twice, once at MIT

00:12:05.369 --> 00:12:09.319
and again when Tony explains to Steve Rogers
that Tony and Pepper have taken a break as

00:12:09.319 --> 00:12:10.319
a couple.

00:12:10.319 --> 00:12:14.529
Possibly a reference to Gwyneth Paltrow's
Marvel contract being at an end despite rumours

00:12:14.529 --> 00:12:16.990
of her having filmed something for Civil War.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.110
Two other Civil War absentees, Thor and Banner,
also get a mention when Secretary of State

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:27.290
Ross asks the Avengers if they can explain
where those two superheroes are right now.

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.379
The romantic relationship between Scarlet
Witch and Vision in the comics is hinted at

00:12:30.379 --> 00:12:34.639
a little more in the scenes between the two
in this movie, in particular the paprika cooking

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:35.639
scene.

00:12:35.639 --> 00:12:39.070
There's also a Vision easter egg when we see
him contemplating a chess board in the movie,

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:43.759
an homage to the 1971 Avengers cover where
Vision is analysing a chess opening.

00:12:43.759 --> 00:12:48.230
And both characters' powers from the comics
get a lot more screen time in Civil War, with

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:52.459
Vision altering his density and firing optic
blasts from his infinity stone.

00:12:52.459 --> 00:12:56.619
Scarlet Witch's energy manipulation is also
much enhanced and she can even power Vision

00:12:56.619 --> 00:12:58.889
down through multiple levels of a building.

00:12:58.889 --> 00:13:03.019
By the way, Hawkeye's motivational mini-speech
to Scarlet Witch at the Avengers Academy with

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:07.329
the words "you wanna mope, you go to high
school; wanna make amends, get off your ass"

00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:11.250
is a call-back to his motivational speech
to her during the Battle of Sokovia in Avengers

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:12.920
Age of Ultron.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:18.420
It's Sharon Carter's eulogy at Peggy Carter's
funeral that helps Steve Rogers convince himself

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.079
that he's right to oppose the Sokovia Accords.

00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:25.040
Sharon's words about how it's "your duty to
plant yourself like a tree, and say 'no you

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.279
move'" are a nod to Captain America's speech
to Peter Parker in the Civil War comics which

00:13:29.279 --> 00:13:31.029
used the same words.

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:35.129
And likewise it was this speech in the comics
by Cap which helped convince Spider-Man to

00:13:35.129 --> 00:13:39.279
swap sides from being pro-registration to
siding with Captain America.

00:13:39.279 --> 00:13:43.540
The romance between Steve Rogers and Sharon
Carter that was hinted at in The Winter Solder

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:46.399
develops into a kiss between the two in Civil
War.

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:50.069
And Cap's words about the kiss being "late"
is a little callback to his description of

00:13:50.069 --> 00:13:54.149
himself as "the world's leading authority
on waiting too long" in Avengers: Age of Ultron,

00:13:54.149 --> 00:13:57.899
and, of course, is also a nod to to his relationship
with Peggy Carter.

00:13:57.899 --> 00:14:02.100
The Agent Carter TV series also gets a subtle
reference when Cap and Bucky are reminiscing

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:06.470
about trying to win a stuffed bear for a girl
named Dolores, or Dot, at Rockaway Beach.

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:10.549
Dottie Underwood is a Soviet assassin on Agent
Carter who trained at the Red Room Academy

00:14:10.549 --> 00:14:13.790
where Black Widow trained.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:18.220
Black Widow's complicated spy background gets
referenced by Iron Man when he snidely says

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:22.829
to her it "must be hard to shake the double
agent thing, sticks in the DNA" after she

00:14:22.829 --> 00:14:26.199
lets Captain America escape despite being
on Team Iron Man.

00:14:26.199 --> 00:14:30.660
Iron Man's words are also a nod to the Ultimates
series where Black Widow tells her boyfriend

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:34.670
Tony Stark that he shouldn't be shocked that
the former "Russian spy" turned out to be

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:35.839
the traitor they'd been looking for.

00:14:35.839 --> 00:14:39.889
Speaking of Black Widow, she has a run in
with Black Panther's security chief, played

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:42.050
by German actress Florence Kasumba.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:46.069
The security chief is likely one of Black
Panther's all-female bodyguards called the

00:14:46.069 --> 00:14:50.610
Dora Milage in the comics and she orders Black
Widow to "move or you will be moved."

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.819
This verbal confrontation is a nod to the
time in the comics when Black Widow was beaten

00:14:54.819 --> 00:14:58.250
by a group of Dora Milage.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.899
Black Panther's costume can deflect machine
guns bullets thanks to the vibranium which

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:03.660
is woven into his suit.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.589
Black Panther scratching Cap's shield with
his vibranium claws is a visual nod to the

00:15:07.589 --> 00:15:12.309
Captain America annual cover which showed
Wolverine slamming his claws into the First

00:15:12.309 --> 00:15:13.699
Avenger's iconic shield.

00:15:13.699 --> 00:15:16.630
And there are more teases to the upcoming
Black Panther movie.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.589
Everett K. Ross, the US government official,
played by Martin Freeman, has a small role

00:15:20.589 --> 00:15:21.651
in Civil War, although it's likely we'll see
more of him in Black Panther.

00:15:21.651 --> 00:15:26.600
In the comics his job is to liaise with foreign
diplomats in particular T'Challa from Wakanda.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.600
The mid-credits scene with Bucky being treated
at an advanced medical centre reflects the

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:35.319
fact that in the Marvel comics, Wakanda is
a wealthy and technologically advanced nation.

00:15:35.319 --> 00:15:39.480
And the huge Black Panther statue revealed
and misty jungle location is an intriguing

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:43.149
peek at Wakanda in the MCU.

00:15:43.149 --> 00:15:47.929
The airport battle which takes place at Leipzig
airport, is a nod to Baron Helmut Zemo's birthplace

00:15:47.929 --> 00:15:49.509
in the comics in Leipzig, Germany.

00:15:49.509 --> 00:15:54.359
And when Zemo says to Captain America "there's
a bit of green in the blue of your eyes.

00:15:54.359 --> 00:15:59.359
How nice to find a flaw" it's a reference
to Zemo's Nazi background in the comics.

00:15:59.359 --> 00:16:03.429
The two days that it took for Zemo to find
the bodies of his wife and child mirrors the

00:16:03.429 --> 00:16:07.410
2 days that Wanda and Pietro Maximoff mentioned
they waited in the rubble of a building expecting

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.420
Stark's bomb to go off in Avengers Age of
Ultron.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:14.369
And with Zemo still alive at the end of Captain
America: Civil War and the villain Ulysses

00:16:14.369 --> 00:16:18.689
Klaw introduced in Age of Ultron, there's
a hint we might get Zemo's Masters of Evil

00:16:18.689 --> 00:16:20.350
in future movies.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:24.600
By the way, the Sokovian kill squad "Echo
Scorpion" that Zemo used to run could be a

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:28.910
nod to the Marvel villain Scorpion who was
also a member of the Masters of Evil.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:35.549
Stan Lee gets a fantastic cameo delivering
a FedEx parcel from Steve Rogers to a certain

00:16:35.549 --> 00:16:38.910
"Tony Stank" which Rhodey makes sure Tony
won't ever forget.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:42.220
This isn't Lee's first cameo as a postal worker.

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:47.799
In the 2005 Fantastic Four movie, Lee appeared
as mailman Willie Lumpkin from the comic books,

00:16:47.799 --> 00:16:50.620
welcoming the Fantastic Four back to the Baxter
Building.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:56.110
Talking of cameos, director Joe Russo, has
a cameo as Dr Theo Broussard who Zemo kills

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.250
leaving the body in his hotel room bath.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:03.189
Joe Russo also played Dr Fine in Captain America:
The Winter soldier and he's credited in both

00:17:03.189 --> 00:17:05.789
films under the alias Gozie Agbo.

00:17:05.789 --> 00:17:10.140
By the way the name, Theo Broussard is likely
a nod to producer Stephen Broussard who is

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.550
executive producer on Doctor Strange and was
also producer on Captain America: The First

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:16.980
Avenger, Iron Man 3 and The Incredible Hulk.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:22.459
Ann Russo, the wife of director Anthony Russo,
is the voice of Zemo's wife who we hear on

00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:23.459
Zemo's voicemail.

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:28.559
And Chris Evans's assistant, Josh Peck, cameos
as a GSG9 Task Force Gunner, while his stand-in

00:17:28.559 --> 00:17:34.110
from Age Of Ultron and The Winter Soldier,
Brent McGee also cameos as a GSG9 Task Force

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.110
Pilot.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:38.900
And Damion Poitier who played Thanos in The
Avengers post-credits scene cameos as one

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.070
of Crossbone's mercs.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:44.420
By the way, the main cast credits which appear
before the mid-credits scene in Wakanda include

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:48.320
some cool artwork with thematic shadows that
relate to the characters.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:52.769
Chris Evans has a Captain America shield;
Tom Holland has a spider's web for Spider-Man;

00:17:52.769 --> 00:17:57.160
Jeremy Renner has arrows for Hawkeye; Chadwick
Boseman has a pattern from his Black Panther

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:02.210
suit; Emily VanCamp has the number 13 for
Agent 13; Paul Rudd has the Pym Technologies

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:06.049
symbol for Ant-Man; and Frank Grillo gets
a large X for Crossbones.

00:18:06.049 --> 00:18:10.970
So guys, what other easter eggs and references
did you spot in Captain America: Civil War?

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.250
And which was your favourite?

